body:
Thanks to [leanmendoza](https://github.com/leanmendoza) for posting this issue. I should have posted it earlier, but kept postponing it. So, I started working on a project with version 4.3 and wanted to try KTX2 textures because of their strong compression, which makes them much faster to work with in VRAM. For reference, I’ll leave this [here](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sYzkwQvaFSc).

This issue actually reproduces up to the current v4.6.dev4.official [bd2ca13c6]. The problem is that the color appears darker as if in sRGB mode, even though it isn’t, which strongly distorts the color, making it darker. This is not a problem with the original textures; for example:

<img width="696" height="470" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3bd0f63a-df48-45aa-8760-4ef87479098e" />

Here you can see the texture has been distorted as if under sRGB, and you can also see that my textures, which were originally made for 4.3, are still distorted the same way.

I also verified that the texture was originally fine:

<img width="732" height="735" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/495dc506-8019-4f9c-9c5b-5ccb3b190ffd" />

This can also be seen on models inside the game. Additionally, brightening textures to work around this issue will break your color palette and distort colors due to certain palette characteristics.

---

I also want to add a bit of code I made on the fly, which will help work around this bug for now:
```gdscript
func linear_to_srgb(image: Image) -> Image:
	var img = image.duplicate()

	#
	if img.is_compressed():
		img.decompress()

	var w = img.get_width()
	var h = img.get_height()

	for y in h:
		for x in w:
			var c = img.get_pixel(x, y)
			#
			c.r = pow(c.r, 1.0 / 2.2)
			c.g = pow(c.g, 1.0 / 2.2)
			c.b = pow(c.b, 1.0 / 2.2)
			img.set_pixel(x, y, c)

	return img
```
This option is fast but less precise.

```gdscript
func linear_to_srgb(image: Image) -> Image:
	var img = image.duplicate()

	if img.is_compressed():
		img.decompress()

	var w = img.get_width()
	var h = img.get_height()

	for y in h:
		for x in w:
			var c = img.get_pixel(x, y)
			#
			c.r = 1.055 * pow(c.r, 1.0/2.4) - 0.055 if c.r > 0.0031308 else 12.92 * c.r
			c.g = 1.055 * pow(c.g, 1.0/2.4) - 0.055 if c.g > 0.0031308 else 12.92 * c.g
			c.b = 1.055 * pow(c.b, 1.0/2.4) - 0.055 if c.b > 0.0031308 else 12.92 * c.b
			img.set_pixel(x, y, c)

	return img
```
And this is a more precise option.

*Visually, in most cases, you won’t notice a difference, especially on bright, high-contrast textures. The difference becomes noticeable on dark, nearly black pixels and in precise color calculations (for example, in lighting and linear PBR rendering).*

Essentially, since Godot treats KTX as already sRGB, we just convert it to linear manually. Using gdext this would be even faster, but it would have to be done somewhere in advance until this bug is fixed. *I also want to point out that if you use this code, you lose all the advantages of KTX2 due to manual decompression, but it can still be useful.*

